WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
 Hello and welcome to Chapter 3, Conditional Execution. In Conditional Execution, we meet

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.260
 the if statement. The if statement is where Python can go one way or another way and it's

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:17.280
 the beginning of sort of our way of making Python make decisions for us. Sequential code,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.960
 we just, you know, do some things. Sometimes that's useful. But now we can have our code

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.580
 check something and then make a decision based on that thing. So the conditional steps in

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:34.000
 Python are pretty straightforward. The key word that we're going to use is the if statement.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.280
 And so if is a reserved word. And the if statement has as part of it a question that it asks.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.080
 And this is asking if x is less than 10. And the colon is the end of the if statement.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.560
 And then we begin an indented block of text. And the way this works in this particular

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:55.160
 thing is this line is the conditional line. If the question is true, the line executes.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.480
 And if the question is false, the line is skipped. And you can think of it the way this

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.840
 is, right? x is 5, ask a question. Is it 10 or not? These questions do not harm the value

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:10.800
 of x. If it is, then we run this code and then we sort of rejoin here and then we test

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.000
 this next if. And if that's true, we do this code and then we do there. But in this case

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.480
 it's going to be false because x is not less than 20 and so it just continues down here.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:27.080
 So if we look at how this works, it runs, it runs this line. Then it sees this question

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:32.000
 and it skips that line. So this line does not run. And so smaller prints out and funny

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:39.080
 prints out. OK? So that's the basic idea of an if statement. And the indentation, when

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.640
 we are done with an if statement, we de-indent back and there's this little block. This is

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:49.200
 one sort of if statement and this is another if statement. And these are the two conditional

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.720
 lines that either run or they don't run, depending on the question, the answer to that question.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.600
 So we have a number of different comparison operators that we can use to ask these true-false

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:06.120
 questions that say, is this true? So again, we're kind of limited to the keys that were

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:14.000
 on computer keyboards in the 1940s and 1950s. Less than, less than or equal to. So we didn't

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.920
 have fancy math characters so we just concatenated less than equal to be less than or equal to.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.560
 This double equals is the asking, is this equal to? So that's a little tricky. The equal

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.840
 sign is that assignment operator. If I was building a language today from scratch, I

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.280
 would probably make assignment be arrow and the equals question to have an equals. Or

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:42.600
 I might say, somewhere I would say question equals. But I'm not writing this, building

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:49.000
 this language, so that's not up to me. So this is the question. Double equals is asking

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.600
 the question is equal to. Greater than or equal? Greater than and not equal. So this

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.580
 is the exclamation point is sort of like not equal. So that's sort of not equal. So that's

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.240
 how we do not equal. So if we take a look at some of these in some examples, all of

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:11.080
 these are going to be true because of the way x is set. If x is equal to 5, that's the

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:16.560
 question version, that's true or false. It'll execute that. If x is greater than 4, it's

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.720
 going to execute that. If x is greater than or equal to 5, it's going to execute that.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.400
 Here's kind of a shorthand where if there's only one line in this block, you can kind

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.840
 of pull it up right on the same line after the equals. If x is less than 6, which it

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.640
 is, true, execute that. Then if x is less than or equal to 5, do that. And if x is not

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.420
 equal to 6, do that. Now, like I said, all these questions have been carefully constructed

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.720
 so that they're true just to kind of show you the syntax of those comparison operators.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.880
 Now, you don't just have to have a single line of text in the indented block, and this

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:54.680
 will be something you're going to get used to. So if we indent more than one line, then

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:01.000
 the indented conditional code is actually these three lines. So the idea is you have

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.120
 an if statement, you come in, you do an indent, and as long as you stay indented, you stay

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.840
 in that if block. If it's false, it just skips all of those. So the way this is going to

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:20.560
 execute, x is 5, we print before 5, is x equal 5? That's the question mark, and that's true.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.460
 So it's going to run all these, and then come back, and then continue on, and then deindent,

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:31.160
 so all this stuff is running, right? And then it says x equals 6. Well, that was false,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:38.240
 so that skips all of them. So none of these lines of code run, so these actually don't

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:44.440
 run, and it says afterwards 6, so that's a mistake. Those don't run right there, because

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:53.000
 x is not equal 6. And so here is a bit of code, it's got some nested, it's got a nested

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.200
 block, but it gives you the sense that you have to be very explicit when you're reading

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:03.240
 Python code, of whether the indent is the same between two lines, the same, increased

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.800
 or decreased. And every time you increase it, you mean something, and every time you

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.680
 decrease it, you mean something, and literally if it stays the same, you mean something as

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:17.360
 well. And so if we take a look at this, here we have a line, and the next line has the

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.200
 same indent. This is an if, with a colon at the end, so we have to increase the indent,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:27.320
 and now we're maintaining it. So these two lines are part of that if, but now we have

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:33.520
 deindent it. So whether you choose to deindent this word, or this word, or whatever, where

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:39.880
 you do this deindent affects the scope of how far this if statement lasts. It lasts

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:45.960
 up to, but not including the line, that's deindented to the same level as the if. So

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.680
 this is a deindent, now we have a blank line, which doesn't matter, and we maintain it,

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.880
 now we have a for, which we'll learn about in the next chapter, which is a looping structure,

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:59.640
 let's do a for, a for runs this five times, it has a colon, and it also expects an indented

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.320
 block, now we have what's called a nested block, where we have an if, and a colon, we

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.120
 go into some more. So this is like two indents, right, so these are one indent, and these

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:15.200
 are two indents, and so this is a block within a block, and then we deindent, so that means

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.440
 this print is not part of the if statement, but is still part of the for statement, and

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:26.440
 then we deindent again, and that means this print is on the same level as that for statement.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:31.680
 So if you start thinking about this, you want to be able to start thinking that these blocks

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:37.840
 are the start of the block with the colon line, up to, but not including this line that's

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.800
 been deindented. So the for goes this far, right, the for goes up to, but not including

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:50.040
 the line that's deindented. The if goes up to, but not including the line that's deindented.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:54.880
 So as you do this, you'll sort of mentally start drawing these blocks, and pretty soon

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.040
 you will start constructing them as blocks, and it takes a while, but doesn't take forever.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:17.480
 But in Python, unlike other languages, you have, this is very important and it matters,

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:21.240
 and you can have syntax errors if you get it wrong, because you're really communicating

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:26.960
 the shape and structure of your code using these indents and deindents. We already saw

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.920
 a nested indent, this is a nested if, so you can put an if within an if, and you can go

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:38.760
 as far deep as you want to go, like Russian dolls. And so here we have x=42, if it's 1,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.200
 we indent 1, and then with this next thing we do, these are at the same level of indent,

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:49.200
 but now we see an if, and it has to indent further. So this is like 2 in, 8 spaces, and

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.080
 then, then we deindent back, actually we deindent back too. And so if you watch this, and you

00:07:54.080 --> 00:08:00.840
 take a look at how this works, it runs to here, oops, back up, comes in here, the answer

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.720
 is yes, x is greater than 1, prints this, is x less than 100, well it's 42, so the answer

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:10.040
 is yes, so it runs this and then it kind of continues back to there. And you can also

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:15.560
 think of drawing boxes around this, this is one if box, and then within that if box there

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:21.640
 is another if box. And again, it's the indent, the indent block up to but not including where

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:28.640
 the deindent happens. And this here is like 2 backwards deindents, so it ends 2 blocks.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.120
 So 2 blocks are ended by where we place this. We could move this in, or we could move this

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.240
 out. We could have it all the way into here, we could have it to here, or here, and where

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:43.680
 we put that line depends on how the ends of these blocks are going to work out. So one

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:49.560
 form of a, that's a 1 branch if that we just saw, but then you can also have what's called

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.240
 a 2 branch if. And the basic idea of a 2 branch if is that you're going to come in, you're

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.840
 going to ask a question, and you're going to go one direction if it's yes, and another

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.440
 direction if it's no. We call this an if then else, it's kind of like a fork in the road.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.680
 And the way to think about it is depending on the output of this question, we're going

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.640
 to pick one or two of these, but if we pick one, the other one's never going to happen.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:13.440
 So it's like an either or. We're either going to go one way, or we're going to go the other

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.720
 way, but there is no path where we somehow go boot through both of them. That, that doesn't

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:25.560
 happen. And the syntax that we use for this is what we call the if then else. And so the

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.840
 first part is normal if with an indent, and then we deindent, and then this is another

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.520
 reserved word, else, with a colon, and then we reindent. And so this is really end up

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:41.440
 being part of a whole block here, and the else is the part, this is the part that runs

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:46.440
 if it's false, and this is the part that runs if it's true. The first branch of the if,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.800
 the first indented block is what runs if it's true, and the second indented block is the

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.920
 one that runs if it's false. And so here we go, it just, if x is greater than two, in

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:59.800
 this case it's yes, we're going to print bigger, and then we're going to be all done. And so

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.760
 we do one, and so this one did run, and this one did not run. So basically with an if then

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:09.920
 else, one of the two branches is going to run, but there's no case in which both branches

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:16.000
 run. And again, you sort of draw these blocks around these things mentally, and in this

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.520
 one you sort of take from the if, the else is really part of the block up to, but not

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:26.200
 including that print, which is back indented, deindented back to the same level as the if

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:33.640
 state. This, Python is actually one of the more elegant languages, even though after

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:39.520
 a while this indenting, when you get too far in it gets a little bit complex, but this

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.720
 is a good way to visualize this with these indents. Coming up next we're going to talk

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:47.600
 about some more complex conditional structures.

